  "notes": "West leads the ♠8 against 3NT. The ♠J might as well be played from the dummy at trick 1. When East plays low, South has a second stopper in spades. South has 7 top tricks (1 spade, 2 hearts, 2 diamonds, and 2 clubs).\n\nSouth must try to develop 2 extra tricks in diamonds without allowing East to gain the lead. A spade return from East will quickly sink the contract.\n\nSouth should cross to the closed hand and lead a low diamond. If West produces the ♦Q, he is allowed to hold the trick. South will have 4 diamond tricks and with West on lead, South's spade holding is protected.\n\nIf West follows low on the first round of diamonds, the ♦A is played. South should finesse the ♦J on the second round, not caring if West wins the ♦Q. The contract will be safe as long as diamonds are 3-2.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
